WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
The agent block is the part of the
workflow where language model reasoning

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.100
happens. But to make it reliable, it's
helpful to understand what you're actually

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.640
giving the model. The messages it starts
from, the model you choose, the tools and

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:21.220
skills it can reach for, and the output
other blocks will use afterwards. Once you

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.700
see those pieces clearly, agents become
much easier to design and debug. Let me

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.580
show you how the agent block works. On the
canvas, an agent block looks like any

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.760
other step. A run arrives at its input
handle and a result leaves on the other

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.760
side. But this block has more going on
inside than any other block in Sim. So

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.000
this time, let's follow the data in.
Inside, the agent block is a conversation

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.900
machine with a station for the messages, a
station for the model, and a station for

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.560
what comes out. The first station is the
message stack, and it starts with your

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.180
system message, the standing instructions
that tell the agent who it is and how to

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.060
judge. You can preload the stack with
earlier user and assistant messages, so

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:11.340
every run starts with that history already
in place. And the data we followed in

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.960
lands at the bottom of the stack, as the
newest user message, filled in from the

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.100
start block's input. The second station is
the model, and this is where you choose

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:26.620
the brain that will read the whole stack.
Here, it's Claude Sonnet. And the brain is

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.320
swappable. Sim supports all the frontier
providers, and the block around it never

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.380
changes, so you could hand this exact
stack to any of them without rebuilding

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:40.480
the step. When the run reaches the model,
every message on the stack is sent in, and

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.100
you are billed for what it reads, measured
in tokens. If tools are attached to the

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.480
block, the model can reach out from right
here, calling them mid -run when it needs

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:54.620
more than the stack contains. Then it
writes, The reply streams out word by

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:59.800
word, and the output side of the bill
counts those tokens, too. Everything the

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:04.200
run produced gets packed into the block's
output, the content, the model it used,

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:09.400
the token counts, and any tool calls it
made. And with that, we are back on the

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:13.620
canvas. The output flows onto the next
block, which reads the agent's content

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:19.000
like any other value. So that's the agent
block, one step on the canvas, and a whole

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.580
conversation running inside.
